\caption{The graph of $G_b(z)$ for $b=1, z\in [-2Q,3Q]\x[-5,3], Q=2$}
\label{Gx1}
\end{figure}

For the case $b=1$, from Figure \ref{Gx1} we observe that the successive poles and zeroes have increasing order, due to the fact that $b$ and $b\inv$ coincide, which creates multiple poles. The poles at $z=-n$ and the zeroes at $z=2+n$ both have order $n+1$ for $n=0,1,2,...$


The graph can be made symmetric by considering $S_b(z)$ instead. The reflection properties give
\Eq{S_b(z)=S_b(Q-z)\inv,\tab \over[S_b(z)]=S_b(\over[z]),}
suggesting a full symmetry of the graph along $Re(z)=\frac{Q}{2}$ and $Im(z)=0$. We illustrate the case for $b=0.7$ in Figure \ref{Sx}.

